<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samshvilde</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samshvilde is a ruined fortified town in the Kvemo Kartli region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, above the gorge of the Khrami river. It served as a seat of the Kiurikian kings of Lori in the late 10th and 11th centuries and later as a Georgian royal fortress. Its ruins include long stretches of walls and the 8th-century Sioni church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samshvilde.jpg" alt="Samshvilde" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aleksey Muhranoff · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samshvilde_fortress_(Photo_A._Muhranoff,_2010).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The ruins lie on a rocky promontory at the confluence of the Khrami and Chivchavi rivers, 4 km south of the town of Tetritsqaro, beside a modern village of the same name. They stretch for 2.5 km and are 400 metres wide. The 10th-century Armenian chronicler Hovhannes Draskhanakerttsi explained the name as three arrows, but it is formed from mshvildi, bow, and means a place of the bow. Excavations in 1968–1970 found two layers of the early Bronze Age Kura–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> culture, with a settlement, a burial ground and a circular cult building.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The medieval Georgian Chronicles call the place Orbi, ascribe its founding to Kartlos and say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took it in his alleged campaign in the Georgian lands. From the 3rd century BCE it was the seat of an eristavi, or duke, under the kings of Kartli, and King Archil (c. 411–435) gave it to his son Mihrdat. In the 7th century Anania Shirakatsi named it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> as the three main towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. Smbat I of Armenia occupied it around 888, and Kiurike II surrendered it to King Bagrat IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1064.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> held it until 1110, when Bishop George of Chqondidi took it for King David IV, who granted it to Ivane Orbeli in 1123. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> stormed it in 1178 after the Orbeli revolt. In March 1440 Jahan Shah of the Qara Qoyunlu sacked the city; the historian Thomas of Metsoph wrote that he massacred its people and built a minaret of 1,664 severed heads at the gate. The city never recovered.</p><p>Taken by Lala Mustafa Pasha in 1578 and recovered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-i-of-kartli">Simon I of Kartli</a> in 1583, it remained a peripheral fortress. In 1747 the Muslim Georgian prince Abdullah Beg fortified it against Teimuraz II, whose son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> stormed it in 1749. By the end of the 18th century it was in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is listed in the National Heritage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> as the City-Site of Samshvilde. The citadel stands on the east of the promontory and the city proper on the west, with the walled fortress between. The domed Sioni church is dated to c. 759–777 by an inscription naming the Byzantine emperors Constantine V and Leo IV, though tradition credits Queen Sagdukht. Other remains include a basalt basilica, a church with an inscription naming David IV, palaces, cisterns, baths and a bridge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samshvilde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from fortresses such as <b>Samshvilde</b> and later Lori, controlling routes between Armenia and Georgia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lori">Kingdom of Lori</a></li></ul></section>
